  "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/cd9fa71ea72045881c25c80de4f6d8b294b60d92ea4e4f27170d68849ce31431.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2(x+y).\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $450\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=450.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{450}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =2(x+\\frac{450}{x}) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{900}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{\u2032}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{\u2032}(x)=2-\\frac{900}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{\u2032}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{900}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =900 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =450 \\\\ x & =15\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=15\\sqrt{2}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{\u2033}(x)=\\frac{1800}{{x}^{3}}\\,\u27f9\\,{P}^{\u2033}(15\\sqrt{2})=\\frac{1800}{(15\\sqrt{2})^{3}}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{\u2033}(15\\sqrt{2})$ is positive, we conclude that $x=15\\sqrt{2}$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=15\\sqrt{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{450}{15\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{30}{\\sqrt{2}}=15\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter are $x=15\\sqrt{2}$ and $y=15\\sqrt{2}.$ In other words, the rectangle with the smallest perimeter is a square with sides of length $15\\sqrt{2}\\,cm.$\n\nFinally, the minimum possible perimeter is\n\n$$\n{P}_{\\text{min}}=4\\cdot 15\\sqrt{2}=60\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$",
